    private fun story1() = EnStory(
        storyId = "adv_ulysses_en",
        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Tower", listOf(
                "Stately, plump Buck Mulligan came from the stairhead, bearing a bowl of lather on which a mirror and a razor lay crossed, and he intoned a mock-ecclesiastical blessing over the grey sweet mother of us all, the mighty and forgotten sea.",
                "It was a bright morning in June, the sixteenth of the month, and the world was still new and full of promise, though Stephen Dedalus, who stood at the parapet and watched the water below, could feel only a cold and alienated sorrow.",
                "Buck Mulligan was a medical student, a man of crude humor and immense vitality, and he made a great show of preparing to shave himself, whistling through his teeth as he worked the lather across his jaw.",
                "He called Stephen up to the parapet, calling him 'Kinch' and 'the bard,' and he spoke of the sea with a mocking reverence that was half-gentle and half-cruel.",
                "Stephen was a teacher now, a young man who had returned from Paris with his ambitions unfulfilled and his soul in a state of quiet torment, and he could find no peace in the company of the mocking Buck.",
                "Mulligan shaved with care, and as he shaved, he told Stephen the story of a madwoman who had thrown herself into the sea, and he made a cruel joke about Stephen's own mother, who had died only a year before.",
                "Stephen felt the cruelty of the joke cut into him like a blade, but he said nothing, for he had learned long ago that silence was the safest refuge for a wounded heart.",
                "Inside the tower, the third of their number, an Englishman named Haines, was stirring from his sleep, and he called out to them in a voice thick with the weight of his own country's history.",
                "Haines was a man of scholarship and of unconscious arrogance, a collector of Irish phrases and Irish folklore, and Stephen found his interest in Ireland to be as patronizing as it was sincere.",
                "They went down to the sea to swim, and Mulligan stripped off his clothes and plunged into the water with the joy of a pagan god.",
                "Stephen did not swim, for he had a horror of the water that he could not explain, and he sat on a rock and watched the two Englishmen in the sea with a mixture of envy and contempt.",
                "Haines spoke of his dreams of a Celtic revival, and Stephen listened in silence, thinking of the many ways in which the English had stolen from the Irish even their own language.",
                "After the swim, they returned to the tower and made breakfast, and Mulligan cooked eggs and made tea, and the three men sat together and ate.",
                "An old woman came to the door to deliver milk, and Mulligan made a joke about the milk of the Irish and the Irish faith, and Stephen thought of the deep poverty of his country.",
                "As they ate, they spoke of the day's plans, and Mulligan spoke of his own plans to go to the pubs of Dublin and to drink with friends.",
                "Stephen told them that he would not return to the tower that night, and Mulligan mocked him for his pride, and Haines said that he was sorry, and Stephen left.",
                "He walked along the strand, along the beach where the water lapped against the sand, and he thought of the sea and of his mother and of the many ways in which he had failed them both.",
                "He had refused to pray at his mother's deathbed, and she had died in agony because of his refusal, and he would carry the guilt of this refusal to his own grave.",
                "He felt the guilt of his refusal not as remorse, but as a wound that would not heal, a wound that reminded him always of the price of his own integrity.",
                "He walked and walked, along the sea, along the strand, and the world opened around him in all its confusion and beauty and terror.",
                "The day was beginning, the sixteenth of June, and somewhere in the city a man named Leopold Bloom was waking from his sleep, and the two of them, Stephen and Bloom, would meet before the day was over.",
                "The two of them would meet, and in their meeting, they would discover a kinship that neither of them had ever known.",
                "For Bloom was a wanderer too, a man who had lost his son and who had lost his way, a man who looked for meaning in a world that seemed to have none.",
                "And Stephen was a wanderer too, a young man who had lost his faith and who had lost his mother and who had lost himself in a world that felt alien and cruel.",
                "The two of them would wander through Dublin, through its streets and its pubs and its brothels and its churches, and they would encounter the whole of human life in the span of a single day.",
                "And by the end of the day, each would have found something that he had been looking for without knowing it, something that would change him forever.",
                "But for now, Stephen walked alone, and the sea stretched out before him, vast and indifferent, like a great grey mother who could not hear the cries of her children.",
                "He thought of Icarus, the boy who had flown too close to the sun and had drowned in the sea, and he thought of himself, a boy who had flown too close to the truth and had drowned in doubt.",
                "The morning was bright and the air was soft, and the world was full of possibility, but Stephen could feel only the weight of the past, and the terror of the future, and the endless burden of being alive.",
                "He walked, and the day began, and the story of Ulysses began with him, with his sorrow and his silence and his refusal to kneel before the gods of his own country.",
                "For Ulysses is not a story about heroes, not a story about adventures, not a story about gods and monsters and the great deeds of ancient times, but a story about ordinary men and ordinary women, walking through an ordinary city on an ordinary day.",
                "And in that ordinariness, in the small moments and the private thoughts and the secret sorrows, the whole of human life is revealed, and the whole of human life is celebrated.",
                "Stephen walked on, and as he walked, he thought of the many things that he must do, the many things that he must say, the many things that he must write, if he was ever to become the artist that he dreamed of being.",
                "He thought of his dead mother, and he thought of the sea, and he thought of the 'agenbite of inwit,' the remorse of conscience, that would never leave him alone.",
                "He thought of the 'ineluctable modality of the visible,' the fact that all things that can be seen are seen, whether we will them to be seen or not.",
                "And he thought of the 'ineluctable modality of the audible,' the fact that all things that can be heard are heard, whether we will them to be heard or not.",
                "He walked through the 'signatures of all things,' the marks that the world has left upon itself, and he read them with the eye of a poet.",
                "And as the morning passed into afternoon, he wandered through the city, and the city wandered through him, and the two of them, the man and the place, became one.",
                "And somewhere in the city, Leopold Bloom was walking too, and the two of them, though they did not know it, were moving toward each other, drawn by a force that was greater than either of them.",
                "For in the great design of the world, even the smallest meetings, the most casual encounters, the most insignificant coincidences, are all part of a pattern that is too large for any one life to contain."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "The Wanderings of Bloom", listOf(
                "Leopold Bloom was a man of middle age, a man of ordinary appearance and ordinary habits, and he walked through the streets of Dublin on the morning of the sixteenth of June with the calm certainty of a man who knows his city by heart.",
                "He had prepared breakfast for his wife, Molly, and he had brought her the morning post, and he had listened to her voice, and he had loved her, and he had feared her, and he had wondered whether she was still faithful to him.",
                "He had bought a kidney from the butcher, and he had fried it on the pan, and he had eaten it with a cup of tea, and he had thought about the many small pleasures of a life that most men would consider unremarkable.",
                "He walked to the post office, and he received a letter from a woman named Martha Clifford, a woman with whom he had been carrying on a secret correspondence, a woman whom he had never met.",
                "He read the letter with a mixture of pleasure and shame, for he knew that his correspondence with Martha was a kind of betrayal, though a harmless one, and he did not know how to stop.",
                "He walked to the baths, and he bathed in the warm water, and he thought about the many small things that filled his life, the many small concerns that consumed his days, the many small delights that made his life worth living.",
                "He walked to the funeral of an old friend, Paddy Dignam, and he rode in the carriage with other mourners, and he listened to their talk of death and of God and of the many ways in which we try to make sense of the senseless.",
                "He thought about his own death, and about the death of his father, and about the death of his infant son, Rudy, who had died only eleven days after his birth, and who had left a wound in Bloom's heart that would never heal.",
                "He walked through the streets of Dublin, and he saw the whole life of the city pass before him, the merchants and the beggars and the priests and the prostitutes and the politicians and the poets.",
                "He thought of the many ways in which the city was like a body, with its heart and its arteries and its veins, with its blood and its breath and its secret wounds.",
                "He walked to the office of the newspaper, and he discussed the business of the day with the other men, and he felt the small pleasures of being part of a world that was larger than himself.",
                "He walked to the library, and he thought about the many books that had been written about Ireland and about the many books that had been written about the world.",
                "He walked to the pub, and he drank a glass of wine, and he listened to the men around him talk of politics and of women and of the many small scandals that made the world go around.",
                "He walked to the beach, and he sat on the sand, and he watched a young woman, a woman named Gerty MacDowell, and he felt the small stirrings of desire that even a man of middle age could not entirely escape.",
                "He walked to the hospital, and he visited a young woman who was in labor, and he listened to the cries of the woman and the cries of the men who surrounded her, and he felt the great mystery of birth.",
                "He walked to the brothel, and he saw the young Stephen Dedalus there, and he saw Stephen break a lamp in a moment of drunken rage, and he saw Stephen flee into the night.",
                "He followed Stephen, and he found Stephen lying in the street, and he lifted Stephen up, and he took Stephen to a place where Stephen could rest.",
                "And in that moment, Bloom discovered a kinship with Stephen that he had not known he was seeking, a kinship that made him feel less alone in a world that had become so lonely.",
                "For Bloom had lost a son, and Stephen had lost a mother, and in the loss of their loved ones, they had both lost a part of themselves.",
                "And in their meeting, in their late-night conversation, in their shared cup of cocoa in Bloom's kitchen, they found something that each of them had been looking for, though neither of them could have named it.",
                "Bloom found a son, and Stephen found a father, and though neither of them would ever say these words, each of them knew, in his own way, what he had found.",
                "And so the day ended, and the two of them parted, and Bloom went up to bed, and he lay beside his wife, and he thought about the day, and about the many small things that had happened, and about the many small things that would happen tomorrow.",
                "And Molly, in the darkness, thought about her own day, and about her own life, and about the many men she had known, and about the one man she had married, and about the young man she had loved as a girl.",
                "And she said yes to life, yes to love, yes to the whole great mystery of existence, and her yes echoed through the night, and it echoed through the city, and it echoed through the world.",
                "For in the end, that is all that any of us can do: to say yes to life, to say yes to love, to say yes to the whole great mystery that we can never understand but that we can always embrace.",
                "And so the story of Ulysses came to an end, not with a bang, not with a triumph, not with a great revelation, but with the quiet yes of a woman in her bed, a woman who had known sorrow and joy, a woman who had known love and loss, a woman who had known the whole of human life."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "The Sirens of Dublin", listOf(
                "In the late afternoon, as the sun began to descend over the rooftops of Dublin, Bloom found himself in a pub called the Ormond, where the barmaids, Miss Douce and Miss Kennedy, presided over the counter like sirens over the rocks of a distant shore.",
                "They were beautiful in a way that was both seductive and inaccessible, and they laughed and they sang and they flirted with the men who came to drink, and the men drank and listened and dreamed.",
                "Bloom did not sing, for he had no voice, and he did not flirt, for he had no skill, and yet he lingered, drawn by the music and by the laughter and by the deep human need to be part of a world that was larger than himself.",
                "The singing was not merely singing, but an ancient ritual, a spell cast over the room, a reminder of the many ways in which human beings have tried to charm the sorrow out of their own lives.",
                "Bloom thought of Molly, of the first time he had seen her, of the way she had sung that afternoon in the summer long ago, and he felt the old love rise in him like a tide.",
                "He thought of the ways in which he had failed her, of the many small failures that had accumulated over the years, of the great failure of losing their son, and he felt the weight of his own guilt press upon him.",
                "He thought of the way she had looked at him when they first met, the way she had chosen him above the other men, and he thought of the way she now looked past him, as though he were no longer there.",
                "The singing continued, and the barmaids laughed, and the men drank, and Bloom sat alone in the corner, listening to the music of a world that had become both familiar and strange.",
                "He thought of the many ways in which all human beings are like the men in the pub, listening to songs that remind them of the loves they have lost and the lives they have not lived.",
                "He thought of the way that all of us are, in our own ways, sirens, singing to each other across the vast distances of our separate lives, hoping that someone, somewhere, will hear us and come to us.",
                "And then the singing stopped, and the spell was broken, and the men returned to their ordinary lives, and Bloom returned to his.",
                "He walked out of the pub into the street, and he walked toward the sea, and he stood on the shore and looked out across the water toward the place where the river meets the sea.",
                "He thought of all the rivers that flow into all the seas, and he thought of all the lives that flow into all the deaths, and he thought of the great cycle of existence that binds us all together.",
                "He thought of the 'yes' that Molly would say in the darkness of their bedroom, the 'yes' that would redeem the whole day, the 'yes' that would make sense of everything.",
                "For in the end, that was all that Bloom had, and all that anyone has: the yes of a person who loves us, the yes of a world that accepts us, the yes of a life that is worth living despite everything.",
                "And as he stood on the shore and looked out at the sea, he felt something like peace descend upon him, and he knew, for the first time in a long time, that he was not alone."
            )),
            EnChapter(4, "Circe's Nighttown", listOf(
                "As night fell over Dublin, Bloom wandered into a district of the city that was known for its brothels and for its nightlife, and the streets were lit by the flickering gas lamps, and the air was thick with the smell of perfume and of beer.",
                "He walked into a house where the young Stephen Dedalus had gone, and he found Stephen in a state of drunken frenzy, dancing with the women and shouting at the walls.",
                "Bloom watched over Stephen as though Stephen were his own son, and he tried to calm Stephen, and he tried to lead Stephen away from the dangers that surrounded them.",
                "But Stephen was beyond reach, and Stephen raised his ashplant, and Stephen struck the chandelier, and the chandelier shattered, and Stephen fled into the night.",
                "Bloom followed Stephen into the street, and he found Stephen on the ground, bruised and bleeding, and he lifted Stephen up and he carried Stephen away.",
                "And in that moment, in that act of tenderness, Bloom became something more than a wanderer, became something more than a cuckold, became something more than an ordinary man.",
                "He became a father, or rather the image of a father, the symbol of the love that flows from one generation to the next, the memory of the son he had lost and the hope of the son he might yet find.",
                "And Stephen, though he did not know it, became a son, a son who had lost his mother and who was looking for a father, a son who would never quite find what he was looking for.",
                "The two of them walked together through the dark streets of Dublin, and the city seemed to be transformed, and the ghosts of the past rose up around them.",
                "The ghost of Rudy, Bloom's dead son, appeared before them, a small boy with a smile on his face, and Bloom wept, and Stephen watched, and the night pressed upon them both.",
                "And the ghost of Stephen's mother appeared, and she called out to her son, and Stephen trembled, and he refused once more to pray, and he wept, and he did not know why.",
                "And the ghosts of all the dead of Dublin, the dead of many generations, rose up together, and they filled the streets, and they called out to the living to remember them.",
                "And Bloom and Stephen walked among the ghosts, and the ghosts did not harm them, for Bloom and Stephen were themselves becoming ghosts, were themselves becoming part of the past.",
                "And so the night passed, and the two of them, the father and the son, the wanderer and the poet, walked together until the first light of dawn appeared in the east.",
                "And when the morning came, Bloom took Stephen to his own home, to his own kitchen, and he made Stephen a cup of cocoa, and he talked to Stephen about the many things that mattered and the many things that did not.",
                "And Stephen, in return, sang a song for Bloom, an old song about a girl and a rose, and Bloom listened and wept and did not know why.",
                "And so the night ended, and the two of them, each in his own way, found something that had been missing from their lives, and they did not speak of it, but they both knew.",
                "For in the end, we are all wanderers, all looking for a home, all looking for a father, all looking for a son, all looking for a 'yes' that will make our lives worth living.",
                "And in the meeting of Bloom and Stephen, in the brief union of two lost souls, we see ourselves, and we see the possibility that even in the darkest night, there is a light that will never go out."
            )),
            EnChapter(5, "Penelope's Yes", listOf(
                "Molly Bloom lay in her bed in the darkness of the night, and she thought about the day that had passed, and she thought about the many men who had passed through her life, and she thought about the one man she had married.",
                "She thought about the first time she had seen Bloom, the way he had looked at her, the way she had known, in that very moment, that this was the man she would marry.",
                "She thought about the many ways in which he had disappointed her, the many ways in which he had failed her, the many ways in which he had become less than she had hoped he would be.",
                "And she thought about the ways in which she, too, had failed him, the ways in which she had hurt him, the ways in which she had become less than the woman she had hoped she would be.",
                "She thought about their son, Rudy, who had died only eleven days after his birth, and she thought about the great wound that his death had left in both of them.",
                "She thought about the many ways in which the two of them had tried to heal that wound, and about the many ways in which they had failed.",
                "And she thought about the young man she had loved as a girl, a young man named Mulvey, who had been the first to kiss her, the first to make her feel like a woman, the first to make her feel alive.",
                "She thought about the way she had given herself to him, the way she had opened herself to him, the way she had discovered in his arms the great mystery of love.",
                "And she thought about the way she had felt when she had first looked out over the bay of Gibraltar, where she had been born, where she had spent her girlhood, where she had first begun to dream of the woman she would become.",
                "She remembered the 'yes' she had said to Mulvey, the 'yes' she had said to Bloom, the 'yes' she had said to life itself, and she felt the great affirmation of existence rise up in her like a tide.",
                "And she said 'yes' again, in the darkness of her own mind, 'yes' to the whole great mystery, 'yes' to the pain and the joy, 'yes' to the loss and the love, 'yes' to everything that life had brought her.",
                "And her 'yes' was not a word, it was a river, it was a flood, it was the whole of the sea, and it flowed out of her and into the world and into the whole of creation.",
                "And in that 'yes,' in that great affirmation of life, the whole of Ulysses found its meaning, the whole of the day found its meaning, the whole of human existence found its meaning.",
                "For in the end, that is all that any of us can do: to say 'yes' to life, to say 'yes' to love, to say 'yes' to the whole great mystery that we can never understand but that we can always embrace.",
                "And in that 'yes,' we are all of us, for a moment, redeemed, all of us forgiven, all of us made whole.",
                "Molly said 'yes' and her 'yes' echoed through the night, and the world was made new, and the story of Ulysses came to its end.",
                "For the story of Ulysses is not a story about heroes, not a story about adventures, not a story about the great deeds of ancient times, but a story about an ordinary man and an ordinary woman, living an ordinary life, on an ordinary day.",
                "And in that ordinariness, in the small moments and the private thoughts and the secret sorrows, the whole of human life is revealed, and the whole of human life is celebrated.",
                "And so the story of Ulysses ends, not with a triumph, not with a revelation, not with a great conclusion, but with the quiet 'yes' of a woman in her bed, a woman who has known sorrow and joy, a woman who has known love and loss, a woman who has known the whole of human life.",
                "And with that 'yes,' the whole of the world is redeemed, and the whole of the world is blessed, and the whole of the world is made new."
            ))
        )
    )

