        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Window", listOf(
                "Yes, of course, if it's fine tomorrow, said Mrs. Ramsay, and her words carried across the room like a small promise, a small certainty in a world that was full of uncertainty, a small gift given freely to her son James, who was six years old and who had been waiting for this moment for what seemed to him like years.",
                "But her husband, Mr. Ramsay, who was a philosopher and a man of truth, could not let the moment pass without correcting the promise, and he said that the weather would not be fine, and he said it in a voice that was both gentle and implacable.",
                "James, who had been sitting on the floor cutting pictures out of a catalogue, felt a great rage rise up in him, and he wished, in that moment, that his father were dead, and he knew that this wish was terrible, and yet he could not prevent it.",
                "For James loved his mother with a love that was fierce and pure and absolute, and he feared his father with a fear that was mixed with admiration and with hatred, and the two emotions were so tangled in him that he could not separate them.",
                "Mr. Ramsay was a man who could not lie, even to spare the feelings of a child, and he believed that to lie was to weaken the mind and to weaken the soul, and he believed this with all the force of his great intelligence.",
                "Mrs. Ramsay was a woman who could not bear to see her children hurt, and she believed that to give a child hope was to give him strength, and she believed this with all the force of her great heart.",
                "The two of them loved each other, but they loved each other in different ways, and this difference was the source of all their joy and all their pain.",
                "The house was full of people, for it was the summer, and the Ramsays had brought their children and their friends and their servants to the house on the Isle of Skye, and the house was alive with laughter and with arguments and with the small dramas of daily life.",
                "There was Lily Briscoe, a young woman who painted, and who was painting a picture of Mrs. Ramsay and of James, and who was struggling, as all artists struggle, to capture something that could not be captured.",
                "There was William Bankes, an old friend of Mr. Ramsay, a botanist, a widower, a man of quiet and gentle nature, who loved Mrs. Ramsay with a love that was pure and unspoken and unrequited.",
                "There was Charles Tansley, a young scholar who was staying with the Ramsays, and who was, in the opinion of everyone present, one of the most insufferable people alive.",
                "There was Augustus Carmichael, an old poet, who spent most of his time in the garden, reading and sleeping and dreaming, and who seemed to belong to a world that was not quite this one.",
                "And there were the children, many children, eight in all, and each of them was a world of its own, and each of them loved Mrs. Ramsay in a way that was absolute and unrepeatable.",
                "Mrs. Ramsay was the center of the household, the still point around which everything turned, and she gave herself to the world freely and endlessly, and she did not know how to stop.",
                "She sat at the window, and she looked out at the garden, and she looked out at the sea, and she felt the moment pass through her like a river, and she knew, as she always knew, that the moment would never come again.",
                "She went to the village, and she visited the sick, and she comforted the poor, and she gave of herself until there was almost nothing left to give.",
                "She returned home, and she sat with her family, and she listened to their talk, and she felt the warmth of their presence, and she knew that this warmth was the only thing that mattered.",
                "She went to her room, and she sat alone, and she let the silence fill her, and she felt the weight of her own self, which she could only shed when she was alone.",
                "For Mrs. Ramsay was not merely a wife and a mother, not merely a woman of charity and of grace, but a soul, a solitary soul, that had known suffering and joy and that had made of these a kind of peace.",
                "And as the evening fell, and the lamps were lit, and the family gathered for dinner, Mrs. Ramsay felt, for a moment, that she had achieved the perfect unity that she had always sought.",
                "The dinner was a great work of art, and Mrs. Ramsay was the artist, and she had created, out of the chaos of the day, a moment of order, a moment of beauty, a moment of communion that would last forever in the memory of everyone present.",
                "The candles flickered, and the table was set with silver and with crystal, and the food was cooked to perfection, and the family and the friends sat together and ate and talked and laughed.",
                "And Mrs. Ramsay, at the head of the table, presided over the feast, and she felt, in that moment, that she had achieved something that could not be improved, something that could not be lost, something that was, in its own way, eternal.",
                "And the evening passed, and the guests went to their rooms, and Mrs. Ramsay went to her room, and she sat alone once more, and she felt the strange satisfaction of a woman who has done her work well.",
                "But she also felt, deep within her, the fear of time, the fear of death, the fear of the loss that she knew was waiting for her.",
                "She thought of her children, and she thought of the future, and she thought of the many ways in which the world would change, and she knew that she would not be there to see it.",
                "She thought of her husband, and she thought of the love that she had given him, and she thought of the many ways in which love could be both a comfort and a burden.",
                "She thought of Lily, and she thought of the painting, and she thought of the many ways in which art and life were both the same and different.",
                "She thought of the lighthouse, and she thought of the light that swept across the sea, and she thought of the many ways in which the light was both a call and a warning.",
