            EnChapter(2, "Time Passes", listOf(
                "The summer ended, and the guests went their ways, and the house on the Isle of Skye was left to the wind and to the rain and to the slow decay of time.",
                "The lamps were put out, and the furniture was covered, and the doors were locked, and the house became a place of shadows and of silence and of memory.",
                "The wind came, and the rain came, and the sea beat against the shore, and the house seemed to groan under the weight of the years.",
                "The garden grew wild, and the flowers died, and the walls of the house grew damp with the salt of the sea.",
                "One night, in the darkness, a window was broken, and the wind swept through the empty rooms, and the curtains billowed, and the dust danced in the moonlight.",
                "And then, in the great dark, news came from the world, and the news was not good, and the family was changed forever.",
                "Mrs. Ramsay died, died quietly, died suddenly, died in the middle of the night, and the world, for a moment, seemed to pause, and then continued on its way.",
                "And Prue, the eldest daughter, married, and died in childbirth, and the world, for a moment, seemed to pause, and then continued on its way.",
                "And Andrew, the eldest son, went to the war, and was killed by a shell, and the world, for a moment, seemed to pause, and then continued on its way.",
                "And the house, the house on the Isle of Skye, stood empty, and the wind came, and the rain came, and the sea beat against the shore, and the house seemed to grow older and stranger with each passing year.",
                "The years passed, and the war ended, and the world began to rebuild itself, and the family began to think of returning to the house.",
                "Lily Briscoe, who had painted Mrs. Ramsay so many years before, came back to the house, and she found the old painting still there, and she set up her easel and began to paint again.",
                "Mr. Ramsay, older now, and more frail, came back to the house, and he was still the man he had always been, and yet he was not the same, for he had lost his wife, and he had lost two of his children, and he had lost something of himself.",
                "And the remaining children came back, and the house filled again with voices and with laughter and with the small dramas of daily life.",
                "And there was Cam, the youngest daughter, and there was James, the son who had wished his father dead so many years before, and there was Prue's son, and there was Nancy, and there was Rose, and the family was not the same, and yet it was still the family.",
                "And the years had passed, and the world had changed, and the house was the same, and the light of the lighthouse still swept across the sea.",
                "And Lily Briscoe, sitting on the lawn, and looking at the house, and looking at the sea, and looking at the light, felt the strange presence of Mrs. Ramsay, and she felt the strange absence of Mrs. Ramsay, and she could not tell which was which."
